clean_summary:
This was an excellent read. The interaction problem section especially resonates. We have seen similar dynamics in broader search systems and wrote about it recently.

@jobergum: "You know me as the BM25 guy, but embeddings are cool too. New post from the @HornetDev team just dropped. ANN tuning at 100M scale, covering embedding bias, graph connectivity, and quantization ceiling." 

The linked article discusses tuning ANN indexing, query embeddings, and quantization across 100 million documents, revealing interactions that benchmarks do not predict.

Own your agent harness, own your intelligence, and own your evaluations in open ecosystems. This doesn’t mean starting from scratch; instead, build on an open base harness where you can edit and extend existing primitives like bash, filesystem operations, permissions, and skills support for your task.

Harnesses today, for better or worse, deeply affect agent performance. Owning this layer ensures your agent is optimized for the tasks you and your customers care about, rather than relying on labs or closed harnesses to align with your goals. The world’s builders should be able to openly observe and control their intelligence stack.

@jobergum: "Don't outsource your agent harness."
